================================================================================
Acknowledgements
================================================================================

Anchor is an implementation of ideas developed by others over two decades.
No code is copied from the projects below; they are acknowledged because the
design would not exist without them.

The temporal model follows the Memento framework, RFC 7089, authored by
Herbert Van de Sompel, Michael L. Nelson, and Robert Sanderson. The service
that operated this protocol at the Los Alamos National Laboratory Research
Library closed at the end of 2025.

The citation anchor model follows the W3C Web Annotation Data Model
(TextQuoteSelector and TextPositionSelector).

The multi-stage anchor reattachment strategy is informed by the Hypothesis
annotation client (BSD-2-Clause, Copyright (c) 2013-2019 Hypothes.is Project
and contributors), and by the publicly documented performance failures of
fuzzy quote anchoring in that project. The decision to use bounded
edit-distance matching rather than diff-match-patch follows from benchmarks
published by Robert Knight in the anchor-quote project (MIT).

Citation export follows the Robust Links Specification.

The empirical basis for this work comes from:
  Klein M, Van de Sompel H, Sanderson R, Shankar H, Balakireva L, Zhou K,
  Tobin R (2014). "Scholarly Context Not Found: One in Five Articles Suffers
  from Reference Rot." PLoS ONE 9(12): e115253.

  Jones SM, Van de Sompel H, Shankar H, Klein M, Tobin R, Grover C (2016).
  "Scholarly Context Adrift: Three out of Four URI References Lead to
  Changed Content." PLoS ONE 11(12): e0167475.

Archive fallback interoperates with MemGator (MIT, Copyright Sawood Alam and
Michael L. Nelson), invoked as a separate process. Its user-agent spoofing
option is intentionally not used.
